{
  "summary": "Builds follow-up performance optimizations on top of PR 36895's serialized CScriptWitness representation. It deserializes witness stacks directly into byte storage without intermediate vector-of-vectors allocations, inlines CompactSize decoding for faster element iteration, and caches the offset of the last element for O(1) back() access. It also defers converting witness stacks to std::vector in VerifyWitnessProgram until specific script rules (P2WSH, Taproot) actually require it.",
  "problem": "Even with serialized witness storage, round-tripping through intermediate std::vector<std::vector<unsigned char>> structures and decoding CompactSizes via general stream readers adds unnecessary CPU overhead during block deserialization and iteration. Furthermore, eagerly materializing vector stacks in script validation wastes significant CPU time on transactions with large witness stacks that are never evaluated (such as unknown witness versions, pay-to-anchor, or taproot when flags are unset).",
  "evidence": [
    "Author's benchmarks across synthetic stress blocks show validation/deserialization latency dropping dramatically across successive optimizations: for unknown witness version blocks with 2.0M 1-byte elements, runtime drops from 128ms on master (185ms minimal serialized) to 14ms with all optimizations; an invalid commitment block drops from 101ms on master to 14ms (PR description)."
  ],
